From: ccbench-remote
Subject: [PATCH] cubic/bbr/vegas: stop capping the iperf client at 75 s

src/wrappers/cubic.py, bbr.py and vegas.py each build the iperf CLIENT command
with a hard-coded duration:

    cmd = ['iperf', '-Z', 'cubic', '-c', args.ip, '-p', args.port,
           '-t', '75']

So every kernel-TCP scheme stops sending after 75 s regardless of `-t`. This is
the same defect as patches/0004 (taova's `onduration=1000000`): the wrapper is
never told the experiment runtime, and it enforces a limit of its own.

OBSERVED 2026-11-05, the reference slots of the 1200 s taova smoke, requested
at -t 120:

    _ref00_cubic   datalink log spans 78.8 s   (47507 lines)
    _ref01_cubic   datalink log spans 79.6 s   (43247 lines)

78.8 / 120 = 66%, below src/analysis/plot.py:119's `duration < 0.8 * runtime`,
so both slots would have been thrown away with "Ignoring this run" and written
`{"cubic": {}}` -- the same way taova's hour was lost on 2026-11-04.

Fix: raise the client's own limit to 360000 s (100 h), well past any runtime we
will request, so test.py's clock remains the only thing that ends the flow.
Nothing about TCP Cubic/BBR/Vegas behaviour changes; `-Z <algo>` and every
other iperf argument are untouched.

NOTE for anyone using upstream Pantheon: unpatched, it cannot measure
cubic/bbr/vegas for longer than 75 seconds.

--- a/src/wrappers/cubic.py
+++ b/src/wrappers/cubic.py
@@
-               '-t', '75']
+               '-t', '360000']
--- a/src/wrappers/bbr.py
+++ b/src/wrappers/bbr.py
@@
-               '-t', '75']
+               '-t', '360000']
--- a/src/wrappers/vegas.py
+++ b/src/wrappers/vegas.py
@@
-               '-t', '75']
+               '-t', '360000']
